WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
one of the most notable and exciting  things about final fantasy 7 remake was  the way it updated the original game's  combat system merging the feeling of  menu-based commands and action game  controls stranger of paradise final  fantasy origins does something similar  with a take on the job system that's a  big part of final fantasy history the  ability to switch between combat roles  on the fly allows for a whole lot of  interesting versatility adding tactical  considerations to protagonist jack's  angry shouting slashing and headbutting  during combat we recently got a chance  to play the second playable demo of  stranger of paradise ahead of its  release like the first demo it includes  the chaos shrine level that ends with  players taking on chaos also known as  garland but it also includes another  section that gives a sense of final  fantasy origins take on the series and  how expansive the job system really is  if you want to see more coverage of  stranger of paradise from gamespot make  sure to hit the like and subscribe  buttons below  the new demo for stranger of paradise  sends you back to the chaos shrine the  scene of the original playstation 5 demo  for the game but this time it adds some  new context that demo ended with you  taking on a night the protagonist  characters called garland who they also  think is some kind of evil entity known  as chaos and they really want to kill  chaos in the second demo defeating chaos  triggers a new cutscene that carries the  story a little further forward and  introduces a new character to your party  it turns out that the chaos advent you  had fought wasn't garland or a monster  it was another hero named neon it's uh  convoluted but it turns out that neon is  the fourth prophecied warrior of light  and joins up with the protagonist jack  and his pals to try to kill the real  chaos who neon doesn't really think  exists the demo then took us to a second  level the referent wetlands though we  didn't get much of a story prompt for  this level it seems like it's the first  stop on the party's trip to find a final  fantasy brand crystal that jack and co  need for their crusade against chaos the  interesting thing about referent  wetlands is that as you work your way  through it you'll use special  interactable nodes to change the weather  in the environment you could alternate  the environment between sunny and rainy  with some areas flooding in the rain and  barring your passage and other paths  closing off in the sun changing the  weather to alter the environment had a  more interesting effect though it  changes how some enemies fight you  several of the creatures we encountered  through the course of the wetlands have  electricity based attacks facing them in  the rain created big puddles where their  attacks were more effective but if you  had the forethought to find a node and  change the weather to sunny before a  fight you could take those abilities off  the table before the encounter even  started the main focus of our look at  the demo though was the job system  square enix gave us a chance to play the  referent wetlands mission with all jobs  unlocked and leveled up allowing us to  see how they all work in action a few of  the jobs appeared in the original demo  like swordsman pugilist mage and lancer  in the new demo we saw the addition of  neon as a sword fighter who carries both  a sword and a shield and more advanced  jobs like warrior knight thief and the  classic black white and red mages like  in final fantasy 1 and other ff games  once you advance a given job you can  unlock more varied in-depth versions of  that job you're free to swap jobs  whenever you want and from what we  played it seems like a good idea the  first demo showed off how tough combat  can be in stranger of paradise it takes  a lot of inspiration from other team  ninja games like neo this demo was big  on showing off how the jobs are  effective in different situations you  can equip two jobs to protagonist jack  at a time which you're able to then swap  between on the fly with a single button  press we found that to be great for  combining a melee class and a  spellcaster class which was essential in  the wetlands in addition to various  hungry beasts and electrically charged  reptiles hanging around there were also  elemental creatures that use spells like  fire and blizzard on our group having  the ability to swap to a black mage job  to hit fire elementals with water spells  made taking them down a lot easier but  even if you're not wielding magic you  can use jack's soul shield ability to  absorb a lot of different kinds of  attacks from enemies like their spells  and throw them back at them wielding  different abilities and using them in  different ways showed stranger of  paradise at its best you get a lot of  options for how to fight enemies which  class you pick allows you to focus on  different kinds of attacks and damage  types for instance the sword fighter is  big on direct damage to hp but doesn't  do much to stagger enemies while the  swordsman and the warrior are more about  driving up that stagger meter on foes to  get a finisher off on them but aren't  great at dealing direct damage hot  swapping between jobs means you could  wail on one creature dodge away for a  second then pull out something like your  white mage abilities and cast regen and  haste on your team before going back  into the fray the opportunities for  coming up with your own strategies and  synergies were particularly prevalent in  the referendum wetlands boss fight where  we fought a big metallic orb filled with  elemental energy the orb would  constantly spit out one of two different  elemental bosses a fire-based one and a  water-based one adjusting your strategy  for different situations for blasting  the elementals with different spells or  cutting in close to deal some melee  damage was key because if you let the  orb get the upper hand it might even  spawn both elementals at once with  jack's soul shield ability however it  was possible to block and capture one  elemental's abilities to use against the  other one stranger of paradise also  rewards you for building job affinity  with your characters certain weapons and  armor increase your job affinity as does  spending experience points to level up  that job the more affinity with the job  you have the more bonuses you accrue  allowing you to mix the upshots of one  job with the fighting style of another  you might get stat bonuses like an  agility boost from raising your affinity  as a lancer but higher affinities can  unlock actual job abilities to use even  if you don't have that job equipped we  also saw hybrid jobs like the red mage  which combines some of the black mage's  spell casting abilities with some of the  white mage's healing abilities you can  unlock those jobs as you climb other job  trees it's an expansive system that  gives you a whole lot of different  combat abilities combos and options for  how you want to deal with foes in fact  you might be a little too spoiled for  choice in stranger of paradise the job  system is extremely expansive almost to  the point of being overwhelming swapping  to a new job lets you set combos and  battle abilities and coupled with your  standard melee and spell casting  abilities your dodge your block your  parry mechanics your finishers and your  special lightbringer fighter mode i  straight up forgot about a lot of the  things i could be doing in a fight  executing your special job abilities  usually requires remembering relatively  simple button combos but in the heat of  battle you can never just stop and think  about what you want to do this also made  playing as a mage kind of annoying your  spell casting interface is a radial menu  you adjust with the thumb sticks while  you're charging a spell you have to pay  attention to spell selection spell  charging and spell targeting all at once  there's also stranger of paradise's big  emphasis on loot an rpg staple that  feels like it's been turned up very high  to some degree stranger of paradise is  an action take on final fantasy tropes  the mission screen for referent wetlands  shows the rewards you'll earn for  completing it which suggests you can  replay missions over and over to grind  better gear and increase your stats the  only trouble is you get a ton of loot  all the time i found myself stopping  every few steps to rearrange my gear as  i leveled up that said the gear system  like the job system seems cool and  expansive you're not just thinking about  what gear is giving your character the  best stats but what bonuses you get from  that gear in terms of job affinity  defensive perks and the like there's a  huge amount of customization on hand and  from the looks of things you can return  to levels and grind out better gear over  and over to make yourself an even more  powerful fighter the hints we've gotten  at stranger of paradise's story the  details of which have been minimal so  far are also pretty interesting neon's  introduction as a character who tried to  become chaos just so someone like jack  would have a chaos to kill is a really  weird and fascinating twist on the usual  final fantasy premise it sounds like  we're going to be fundamentally  questioning the idea of warriors of  light and fighting a big bad boss at the  end of a game it also seems like there  might be more than one set of heroes  since neon had a group of her own and we  might see some fundamental questioning  of the prophecies driving these heroes  on their quests my impression from the  second demo of stranger of paradise is  that its combat and systems are fun to  play with and interestingly responsive  building a uniquely final fantasy take  on some of the things we've seen from  other big action games such as neo but  there's also a lot to parse here and can  get confusing that said this spin on  final fantasy mechanics and combats is a  fascinating one and i enjoyed pummeling  my way through various enemies and tough  bosses it's a final fantasy game where  it feels like your strategic choices are  just as important as your quick reflexes  in combat and while everything you know  about final fantasy makes stranger  paradise feel familiar there's a lot  more going on under the surface of what  we've seen so far  [Music]  you

